Here's one possible implementation in JavaScript:

```javascript
function powerOfSix(n) {
  // Check for invalid input
  if (typeof n !== 'number' || n < 0) {
    return false;
  }
  // Calculate the logarithm base 6 of n
  const log6 = Math.log(n) / Math.log(6);
  // Check if the result is close to an integer
  return Math.abs(log6 - Math.round(log6)) < 1e-10;
}
```

The function first checks if the input is a non-negative number. If it's not, it returns false. Then it calculates the logarithm base 6 of the input using the formula:

```
log6(n) = log(n) / log(6)
```

This gives us the exponent of 6 that corresponds to the input. For example, if `n` is 36, `log6(n)` is 2 because 36 = 6^2. If `n` is not a power of 6, the logarithm will be a decimal number.

Finally, the function checks if the difference between the calculated logarithm and its rounded value is very small (less than 10^-10). If it is, the input is a power of 6 and the function returns true. Otherwise, it returns false.

This algorithm has O(1) time complexity because it only involves basic arithmetic and logarithmic operations, which are computationally cheap. It also avoids brute force methods like iterating through powers of 6, which would take O(log n) time at best.

