Here's an implementation of insertion sort in C++:

```
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void insertionSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for(int I = 1; I < n; i++) {
        int key = arr[i];
        int j = I - 1;
        while(j >= 0 && arr[j] > key) {
            arr[j + 1] = arr[j];
            j--;
        }
        arr[j + 1] = key;
    }
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {5, 2, 6, 1, 9};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    insertionSort(arr, n);

    cout << "Sorted array: ";
    for(int I = 0; I < n; i++) {
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

The `insertionSort` function takes in an array of integers `arr` and its size `n`, and sorts the array in ascending order using the insertion sort technique. It works by iterating through the array one element at a time, and for each element `arr[i]`, it compares it to the elements before it (starting from `arr[i-1]` and moving leftwards), and shifts any larger elements to the right to create space for `arr[i]`. Once the right position for `arr[i]` is found (either because all the elements before it are smaller, or because it has reached the beginning of the array), `arr[i]` is inserted in that position.

In the `main` function, we create an example array `arr`, and call `insertionSort` to sort it. We then print out the sorted array.

