Here's the code for finding the length and longest common subsequence of two input strings using dynamic programming in C++:

```
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
  string s1, s2;
  getline(cin, s1); // read the first string
  getline(cin, s2); // read the second string

  int n = s1.length(), m = s2.length();

  // create the DP table
  int dp[n+1][m+1];

  // initialization
  for (int I = 0; I <= n; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j <= m; j++) {
      if (i == 0 || j == 0) {
        dp[i][j] = 0; // length of LCS with empty string
      } else if (s1[i-1] == s2[j-1]) {
        dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i-1][j-1]; // add 1 to length of LCS
      } else {
        dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]); // take the maximum of previous values
      }
    }
  }

  int len = dp[n][m]; // length of LCS
  cout << len << endl; // output the length

  // construct the LCS from the DP table
  string lcs = "";
  int I = n, j = m;
  while (i != 0 && j != 0) {
    if (s1[i-1] == s2[j-1]) {
      lcs = s1[i-1] + lcs;
      i--;
      j--;
    } else if (dp[i-1][j] > dp[i][j-1]) {
      i--;
    } else {
      j--;
    }
  }

  cout << lcs << endl; // output the LCS
  return 0;
}
```

In the code above, we read two input strings using `getline` and then initialize a 2D `dp` table with an extra row and column for handling the empty string cases. We use a nested loop to fill the table according to the recurrence relation:

```
         { 0                                       I = 0 or j = 0
dp[i][j] ={ 1 + dp[i-1][j-1]           if s1[i-1] == s2[j-1]
         { max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])   otherwise
```

After filling the `dp` table, we can read off the LCS from the table by starting from the bottom-right corner and following the arrows based on the values in the table. We construct the LCS while moving towards the top-left corner and output the final LCS.

Note that the time complexity of this algorithm is O(mn), where m and n are the lengths of the input strings, since we fill a 2D table of size (n+1) x (m+1). We can further optimize this algorithm by using only two rows of the DP table at a time, but this would not change the asymptotic time complexity.

