Here is a sample code for a command-line C++ program that prompts the user for a text file path and a word, reads the file and prints out the number of occurrences of the given word (case-insensitively):

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

// Function to count the number of occurrences of a word in a string (case-insensitively)
int countOccurrences(const string& str, const string& word) {
    int count = 0;
    string::size_type pos = 0;

    // Convert both the string and the word to lower case
    string strLower = str;         
    transform(strLower.begin(), strLower.end(), strLower.begin(), ::tolower);
    string wordLower = word;
    transform(wordLower.begin(), wordLower.end(), wordLower.begin(), ::tolower);

    while ((pos = strLower.find(wordLower, pos)) != string::npos) {
        ++count;
        pos += wordLower.length();
    }

    return count;
}

int main() {
    string fileName, word;
    cout << "Enter the file name: ";
    getline(cin, fileName);
    cout << "Enter the word to search: ";
    getline(cin, word);

    ifstream infile(fileName);

    if (!infile.good()) {
        cerr << "Error opening file, please enter a valid file name." << endl;
        return 1;  // indicate failure
    }

    int count = 0;
    string line;
    while (getline(infile, line)) {
        count += countOccurrences(line, word);
    }

    infile.close();

    cout << "The word \"" << word << "\" appeared " << count << " times in the file." << endl;

    return 0;  // indicate success
}
```

In this program, the `countOccurrences` function is used to count the number of occurrences of a word in a string (case-insensitively) using the `find` function of the `string` class. The `transform` function is used to convert both the string and the word to lower case before comparing them.

In the `main` function, the program prompts the user for the file name and the word to search. It then attempts to open the file using the `ifstream` class and checks if the file was opened successfully. If the file cannot be opened, the program prints an error message and returns failure (indicating a non-zero exit status).

If the file is opened successfully, the program reads each line of the file using `getline` and calls the `countOccurrences` function to count the number of occurrences of the given word in each line. The total count is accumulated in the `count` variable. Finally, the program prints the total count of occurrences of the word in the file.

The program returns success (indicating a zero exit status) if it executes without any errors.

